  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d1855e0e8f1b8feed4eb2f5fb9dd2a1abadd0ee92304ae0460f9bd130e376b04.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${W}^{\u2032}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${W}^{\u2032}$ is $r=2.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=\\sqrt{3}$ because $W$ lies $\\sqrt{3}$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $W$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
